    {"d":10.135,"cues":[0.8],"say":["Day 56. H T M L that means something, for browsers, screen readers, search engines, and every device."]},
    {"d":33.607,"cues":[0.3,8.178,16.537,25.71],"say":["Every page starts with the same skeleton. The doctype puts the browser in standards mode, not quirks mode.","The lang attribute on the html element tells screen readers how to pronounce the text, and drives translation and hyphenation.","In the head, charset U T F 8 comes first, then the viewport meta tag, so phones lay out the page at the real device width.","A unique title names the tab, the bookmark, and the search result. The body holds everything users see."]},
    {"d":32.144,"cues":[0.3,6.047,17.274,23.786],"say":["Landmark elements split the page into regions with a known job, like the parts of a book.","Header is the banner. Nav is navigation. Main is the one main content. Aside is complementary. And footer is content info.","Screen reader users jump straight from landmark to landmark, the way you scan a page with your eyes.","Careful. A header or footer inside an article or a section is just that part's header, not a page landmark."]},
    {"d":26.8,"cues":[0.3,9.08,18.8],"say":["Headings are the table of contents. One h 1 for the page title, h 2 for sections, and h 3 inside those.","Never skip a level for looks. Jumping from h 2 to h 4 leaves a hole in the outline. Change the size with C S S instead.","Many screen reader users move by headings first, so this outline is how they read your page."]},
    {"d":32.313,"cues":[0.3,6.12,15.206,23.898],"say":["A div with a click handler can look exactly like a button. It is not one.","A real button is focusable, works with Enter and Space, has the button role, and can submit its form. All for free.","Other native elements do real work too. Details and summary toggle, and a dialog opened with show modal sits in the top layer.","ARIA adds meaning, never behaviour. The first rule of ARIA: use the native element when one exists."]},
    {"d":31.421,"cues":[0.3,8.461,15.832,23.921],"say":["Every control needs a visible label. Link them with the for and id attributes, and clicking the label focuses the input.","A placeholder is not a label. It vanishes as soon as you type, and it is often too faint to read.","Pick the right type, input mode, and autocomplete. Phones show the right keyboard, and browsers can fill it in.","Group related controls in a fieldset with a legend, like the radio buttons for a delivery option."]},
    {"d":35.28,"cues":[0.3,10.73,19.655,27.544],"say":["The browser checks constraints for you: required, type, min, max, and pattern. Each input exposes a validity object.","Style errors with the user invalid pseudo class. It waits until the user has interacted, instead of shouting at an empty form.","On submit, only controls that have a name and are not disabled are sent. Form Data collects exactly those.","Client checks are for comfort. Anyone can bypass them, so always validate again on the server."]},
    {"d":27.059,"cues":[0.3,8.819,18.56],"say":["Give every image a width and a height. The browser derives the aspect ratio, and reserves the space before the file arrives.","Without them, text renders first, and then jumps down when the image loads. That jump is layout shift, measured as C L S.","Alt text says what the image means. A purely decorative image gets an empty alt, so screen readers skip it."]},
    {"d":32.63,"cues":[0.3,7.251,14.46,23.712],"say":["Source set lists the same picture at several widths, and sizes tells the browser how wide it will be shown.","The browser multiplies the display width by the pixel density, and downloads the smallest file that is big enough.","The picture element, with source elements, offers modern formats like A V I F and Web P. The image inside is the fallback.","Lazy load images below the fold. Never lazy load the main hero image. Give it fetch priority high instead."]},
    {"d":14,"cues":[0.5],"say":["Recap. Now open the Day 56 topics below, and run every example yourself."]}
